{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas","id":55108,"title":"Courting New Ideas","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-01-21T18:51:50-08:00","id":55108,"title":"Courting New Ideas","content":"I have run Changeling with three different gaming groups and every time I introduce the courts, it really confuses them. On one hand, your &quot;court&quot; is tied to your mantle and preferred glamor-feeding (you are what you eat, I suppose). But your court is also a political entity for a region, headed by the most powerful of the Lost. But that political group shifts every three months, handing over the crown as the seasons change, so it's really a set of four &quot;power couples&quot; who pass around the responsibility of being CEO, but all of them are on the board of directors (so to speak)? The system seems extremely unstable with the constant switching of who is at the reigns-- how do they get anything done? And that brings up the main question-- what does the court do anyway? If this was an old fashioned aristocracy, they would be training and arming an group so those knights &amp; soldiers could beat up people for their lunch money, or in other words, taxes. If the Lost Courts are a more modern form government, they would be funding public works with taxes-- building roads, collecting trash, keeping up a police force and law courts, etc. So I'm curious, what you do think the Lost Courts do? Do your courts switch heads of state every three months?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T19:02:23-08:00","id":55115,"title":"","content":"They're a support group for a bunch of people who've had their lives thrown out of whack in a way that most of human society can't really understand and the structure exists to try to capture some of the feel of Arcadia without the constant gaslighting and horrifyingly unchecked power imbalance. &quot;Getting things done&quot; is <i>tertiary</i>. (Blending in with mortal society and keeping everybody mostly functional is secondary. &quot;Finding everyone something to do&quot; is part and parcel of that primary point, given that just about everybody had to break out of a role to get back to Earth and being stuck in that in-between phase is not great for Clarity even in a place where the world isn't likely to visibly change in the blink of an eye because a mad god-beast thought it would be funny.)<br /> <br /> The cycling leadership is insurance against this relatively safe space becoming yet another dreamlogical domain and also gives the Lost hope for the next season if this season's Monarch is particularly unpleasant but a revolt isn't in the stars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 01-21-2014, 10:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T19:59:43-08:00","id":55153,"title":"","content":"I'm very new to the system and CtL, but I think there is a lot of potential 'deep narrative' in the higher purposes of the Courts. While it could be that Courts would turn with the seasons that are mundane, my sense of story would be that this is not what actually happens.<br /> <br /> As the fae clock seems to run slower, and the powers represented by the Courts are also a philosophy, and/or if you are using some of the 'foreign courts' of other continents........... I'd not expect to rotate rulers every three months. I'd expect magic to be turning those wheels, not the calendar, and it would be hard for the PCs to put their finger on the pulse of those changes. Further, it might involve secrets that someone with High Wyrd, or very specific Social skills might learn about.<br /> <br /> It could also be entirely like Courts of old, where a large part of the Court's duties is preparing to resist incursions of the Fair Folk. Who is to say that there are not constant tricks built/moved/added to the Hedge, or some spying on deep Fair Domains, to see what abuses are being done?<br /> <br /> I could see one campaign where the Courts are like the NSA or magic rumors.... another where live combat is going in deep in the Hedge with terrible Dream Monsters.... another where Goblins and various low power races are actually allies with the Lost Courts, in some hope that centuries of abuse by the Fair Folk might some day end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Arref</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>......making magic since 1154 B.C.</i></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","name":"Arref","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T20:03:43-08:00","id":55155,"title":"","content":"One purpose of the Courts is to befuddle the Gentry. Keepers are inherently static and greedy creatures. The act og voluntarily turning over power without a fight is completely alien tot eh Keepers\u2019 mindset. It makes the changelings less Arcadian, and thus harder for the Gentry to home in on. When Courts stop power-sharing, they become more like the Gentry, and thus easier to ferret out.<br /> <br /> Also, they are not really governments, but more like clubs or mutual-aid societies. Yes, there are rules, and you pay dues if you are able, but those can be in favors or aid, not necessarily in money. Courts definitely aren\u2019t going to open hospitals. If a changeling needs a job, and you own a business, maybe he becomes a night watchman or a clerk in some essentially made-up department with a generic name and a murky place in the corporate org-chart. Remember, changelings are profoundly broken people with enough idiosyncrasies to fill the Pacific; herding cats doesn\u2019t even begin to describe it. Cajoling and just being there to listen is going to work much better than penal codes and tax breaks.<br /> <br /> Changelings also know the schedule for turning over power. I imagine non-time-critical decisions get put off until the appropriate Court is in power. Unless someone needs to be taken out now, leg-breaking would be dealt out mostly in the summer months. On the other hand, Courts don\u2019t just dissolve in the off months, so if some person just needed to be put in the hospital, the Winter Queen might ask the Summer King to pretty please send some of his courtiers with anger-management issues to pay said person a visit.<br /> <br /> And always remember that the other Courts are not the enemy. Courts may give each other grief, and try to one-up each other to a certain extent. But the enemy is the Gentry. No one goes so far as to hamstring their allies enough to let the Gentry profit. It\u2019s like a big clannish family. We may slap each other around every once and awhile, but we will beat you senseless if you slap one of us.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1651-phalamir","name":"phalamir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1651_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"143"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T20:28:56-08:00","id":55168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>phalamir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=55155#post55155\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Changelings also know the schedule for turning over power. I imagine non-time-critical decisions get put off until the appropriate Court is in power. Unless someone needs to be taken out now, leg-breaking would be dealt out mostly in the summer months. On the other hand, Courts don\u2019t just dissolve in the off months, so if some person just needed to be put in the hospital, the Winter Queen might ask the Summer King to pretty please send some of his courtiers with anger-management issues to pay said person a visit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Winter is probably perfectly capable of handling that themselves, as are the other courts. It would be a rare Court that wouldn't have someone capable of being menacing or taking someone out, and Winter are famed for their assassins. The four courts aren't just party-goers, scholars, sneaks, and fighters. I prefer to view them in terms of how they choose to define their relationship with the Gentry. Winter hides, Summer fights, Autumn studies, and Spring prefers to throw itself deep into the human side of the world, but none of this means that you couldn't find a Spring Court thug (he just wouldn't be as interested in fighting the Gentry, just other humans) or a Summer scholar (he'd just be more interested in the practical applications than an Autumn colleague might be). Events have to be a bit more on the large scale side before I see them working in the way you describe (Winter is entirely capable of putting someone in hospital, but if a war against the Gentry is looming...).<br /> <br /> As to the seasonal rotations, every three months is exactly how it works in my Freehold. The one possible exception is that the other three courts have little respect for Winter (which has fewer than half the members of the others), where it's become accepted that Freehold issues are voted on by all four Kings and Queens, with Winter generously being allowed to break ties.<br /> <br /> This all sort of ties into the OP's question. The principle role of the Court Leaders and the Freehold is to take care of its members. Each Court has a different philosophy, but the voluntary giving up of power helps to keep them hidden from their old captors. Most of the time, the main job of the Freehold is not to have Keepers or mortal authorities noticing and mucking with their business. I quite like Satchel's response as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-21T21:10:01-08:00","id":55204,"title":"","content":"I think the way in which the Courts are presented in the rulebooks are confusing as well. As presented, the Seasons are metaphysically philosophical support groups as well as political organizations that govern matters of supernatural and secular importance. They speak of the unifying nature of freeholds and then speak of the Courts as quarrelsome clans bent on the subjection of their peers. What the hell? In Rites of Spring, it is written that changeling freehold organization varies greatly depending on culture and region ... and then Lords of Summer goes and contradicts it. Ugh.<br /> <br /> For me, the Seasonal Courts are not really a political entity. It is more like a philosophy/religion that just happens to have the benefit of being backed by one of the fundamental forces of Nature. One's Mantle is not representative of that changeling's status or relevance in freehold culture, but rather it is a sign of that changeling's particular devotion to the precepts and ideals of their Season. The benefit of high Mantle, besides being able to shoot lasers out of your eyes and what not, is that one's greater connection to the earthly Seasons strengthens their connection to the real world, making it difficult for fairies to spy on or manipulate them. Areas that have high concentrations of Seasonal courtiers (freeholds) tend to have lower instances of fairy mischief and malice due to the power of so many Mantles gathered in close proximity. <br /> <br /> Of course, since changelings are people, they'll want to organize in some way. This is especially true given the nature of the threat that all changelings are running from. Since birds of a feather flock together and all that jazz, changelings group themselves with other folks that share their beliefs and opinions. However, the manner in which the Seasonal courtiers organize depends on a lot of factors. But the manner in which a Freehold is run, ultimately, has more to do with the ambitions and motivations of the changelings who are a part of it and less with their Seasonal alliances. Some freeholds symbolically give power to courtiers when the Season of their affiliation rolls around, and everyone tries to adopt some of the traditions of that belief system until the weather starts to change. But not everyone feels so compelled by faith, even magic fairy faith, and some will act against the ruling Courts interests. However, it is usually best not to mess with courtiers when their Season is in power, as strange weather phenomena and strange destinies become more common when courtiers of the ruling Season get messed with.<br /> <br /> So what do the Lost Courts do? Well, they do whatever you want them to do. Some freeholds may be organized like a mafia syndicate, another may be centered around a popular downtown nightclub scene. Changeling courtiers color their lives with their Seasonal alliances, they are not ruled by them<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-22T19:41:13-08:00","id":55903,"title":"","content":"I'd like to thank everyone for participating so far-- everyone has brought something different to the table and that is really helping me think through my own ideas and assumptions more fully as well as adding to the mix. I keep rolling around Satchel's and Phalamir's ideas of the Court as support group-- I admit that I never quite thought of it that way because I was imagining that the motley was a social/support group and the courts were something else-- a more political something. If the Courts worked in strong support capacity, it would fundamentally change how I introduce them to the characters-- they would be ready to show up very early to help though the transition to the mortal world. <br /> <br /> Currently, my version of Changeling courts are running on a few key ideas-- I understand that my version is not really that close to the rule book version. <br /> <br /> Power in any given court rests on four high Wyrd ranking pairs (they are not necessarily couples, but they are allies). Each couple is important because others of their season respect them and feel they are affiliated with them in a basic way. One couple is ascendant, or Regent and will remain so, like a British Prime Minister, until there is a lack of confidence and it goes to a vote. This means that an area might have a set of rulers for fifty years, or maybe for just one. It also means that if a region attracts more of a certain type of season, because the climate (both meteorological and social), then one seasonal group will have an advantage. For example, there are a lot of Summer Lost in Las Vegas because of the hot climate, but the vice centered economy also means that Spring Lost are found there aplenty. Those two groups tend to hand power back and forth. There are some Autumnal and Winter Lost there, but their voting block tends to be smaller. <br /> <br /> I think my most non-canon idea is that Courts and Freeholds are two different things. I have a map of North America that is broken into territories defended by Changeling courts. There are places left out of the map-- Detroit for example is not held by any Changeling court because Vampires are so dominant there that the turf is not claimed. The Courts are devoted to holding territory, squabbling occasionally with other Changelings for territory, fending off Keepers, ferreting out puppets, and enacting some ritual magics for the seasons. Freeholds are city-states with that are neutral politically and only hold a tiny territory. They offer places for courts to meet and confer, people from different courts to do business, and a place to live that is less dominated by seasonal politics and wyrd power. Freeholds provide community to those on the outs with Lost in power at a court, but also give someone with high Wyrd but no desire to rule a place to live in peace.<br /> <br /> TBC....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T08:59:48-08:00","id":56171,"title":"","content":"So, what has led to this organizational structure being the &quot;standard&quot; for freeholds of the Seasonal Courts? Is there some sort of &quot;meta freehold&quot; government that imposes these standards on all changeling groups that wish to organize along the lines of the Great Courts? Or maybe it is part and parcel of the agreement that one makes upon swearing themselves to a Season? But if that is the case, who enforces these standards? Are there Seasonal &quot;Whips&quot; who travel around making sure that Seasonal Freeholds tow the party line? Is it all done with Oaths? To bring up one of your initial questions, with all of these freeholds working on a federated level ... what is it that Courts actually do? How do Crowns manifest? Is there a special Regency Crown? Or maybe it sorta works like the Charmed Circle? I am curious to know more about your take on the Courts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-23T19:57:50-08:00","id":56543,"title":"","content":"Firefly Night has very good questions-- really they are part of the heart of why I asked about everyone's take on the system in the first place. I have some answers that I've come up with in my game, but I continue to be interested in other people's take on why courts are what they are and do what they do.<br /> <br /> Essentially, Firefly has hit on my answer: my version of the court structure exists because of the Seasons. In order to anchor themselves to the mortal realm and prevent the Keepers from taking them back or overrunning the world, the Changeling Courts perform rituals that enrich the Seasons, generating power by creating the right emotions in the mortal populace.<br /> <br /> Every court needs representatives of all four seasons (which also represent the most primal emotions) to enact the Solstice and Equinox rituals properly, which create barrier that force the Gentry to follow rules in this world that they do not have to in their own. The highest mantled people of any given area are both needed and to some extent driven by their pacts with their Season to enact these rituals. Others of their season are attracted to these events to both Harvest but also because they affirm their connections to the mortal realm. <br /> <br /> The people that are called to work the rituals are always one male and one female because the Seasons and the mortal realm in general favor the complimentary pair. We see this in dualities of death &amp; birth, night &amp; day, and in the realm of contracts the \u201ceternal\u201d and \u201cfleeting\u201d pairs. If the Seasonal magics are left undone, then that region becomes very vulnerable to Keeper invasion, and the mortals and Lost suffer.<br /> <br /> The Courts are not affiliated with one another in any top down system\u2014they fight and squabble for territory like feudal Europe (and marry and ally in the feudal way as well). All of my stuff is pretty big and abstract, how a particular Court actually runs on a day to day basis could differ quite a lot from other Courts as long as the basic purpose is carried out by the top people.<br /> <br /> As for the crowns, while top couple in each season are designated King and Queen, only the Regent couple are in power. The Regents have the Crown; if there is a successful take over, the new couple's Crown will manifest as a result of the combined will of the Lost and the blessing of the Season.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T12:14:38-08:00","id":57561,"title":"","content":"Like phalamir said, the main thing is (even if the changelings don't know it) that Gentry don't understand court system. It doesn't matter what Courts rule, just that the rule changes. (If it has to be the four courts, then Dawn/Dusk and Sun/Mon pairs would be impossible). In Lords of Summer Winnipeg freehold has Winter and Summer court, Summer ruling during the three summer months and Winter during the rest, but the Winter changelings doing certain rituals before and after Summer's rule to emulate the other courts.<br /> <br /> Imagine being in a hobby club, where the ruling party changes every year and the new ruling group decides things without asking from other people. And then imagine that it's not just the hobby, but they also have a word to say about where you live, where you work and so on. That's how it is. You are part of the Court, they are part of your life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2095-daider-santo","name":"Daider Santo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2095_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T18:20:43-08:00","id":61214,"title":"","content":"I don't know if anyone is still interested in this thread, but I have a follow up question for everyone: how many changelings do you typically imagine are in a typical court or freehold? I know that the book emphasizes that there are a lot of different freeholds, but the one you use most often, is it made up of about twenty to thirty changelings, a couple hundred? I understand that there may be more Lost where there is more glamor to feed on, so do you think of the changeling population as about 1% of a given city's population? Or 10%?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T18:38:49-08:00","id":61227,"title":"","content":"The freehold of my last Changeling game, set in a fictitious large metropolitan area, had somewhere between eighty and 120 changelings, all-told, spread between various businesses and assorted other groups - the Summer Court had a sizable presence in the local Presbyterian church, Autumn had its fingers deep in the local historical society, and Spring and Winter held ownership of two different drinking establishments, among other things. City population overall was a few hundred-thousand people, but the influx of new people was comparatively low year-to-year - the fallout of a major conflict with a fledgling Fae in the mid-50s led to a bit of supernatural damage-control and now the place is slowly hemorrhaging people every couple of years or so, much to the consternation of the local vampires.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T20:52:26-08:00","id":61284,"title":"","content":"My game is set in Brisbane, Australia, so the city has a mortal population of about two million. There are a total of 30-odd Changelings in the city. The Spring Court is close to half the population on its own, Autumn and Summer are roughly even, and Winter has just 5 members. The entire Freehold is clustered in around the CBD, having retreated to an area that was traditionally Spring territory following a dust storm (cover for a Fae attack) a few years ago. Spring was the only Court to come through relatively intact, thanks to the powers of their high Wyrd Oracle Child-Queen. New Changelings don't emerge very often (the PCs were the first arrivals in the four years since the Fae attack, but there have been a few more arrivals in the six months since then), and there is a general siege mentality. The Summer Court are pretty much all hunkered down in a fortified Hollow, the Autumn Court owns a few businesses in the local China Town and has strong roots in both criminal gangs and the police force, and the Spring Court mostly revolves around a small hotel. Oh, and one member owns a brothel.<br /> <br /> For me, Changeling populations are much more tightly dictated by the availability of trods than the amount of Glamour available (Glamour doesn't take anything from the person supplying it, so it's hardly a limited resource). In my game, the population is kept on the low side by a few nasty Fae that have been locking down the local trods, but when their alliance crumbled Changelings started to trickle through again. I could easily see a massive Changeling population in a small town of a few thousand people, so long as there were plenty of relatively safe trods nearby.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T21:12:33-08:00","id":61289,"title":"","content":"I tend to like smaller freeholds. <br /> <br /> For Ex: My Bay Area game had about 200 changelings scattered about the region, split up between five freeholds. In a region of about 7 million people, that is roughly .003%. The largest freehold (San Francisco's Mission) had about 35 changelings and the smallest was a Directional Court freehold in the East Bay that boasted 4 courtiers (although they had a large number of hobgoblin and bewitched human servants). I just find the number to be more manageable from a GMs perspective. <br /> <br /> I don't want to lessen the rarity of changelings by having a vast Lost under-culture, and I have always preferred running my World of Darkness games with a strong human component. It is so easy to run games where all the interactions between characters and NPCs consist of supernatural liaisons, like an old school all vampire soap opera.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T21:44:58-08:00","id":61305,"title":"","content":"Oooh, yeah, I've been meaning to introduce the Freehold of the neighbouring city sometime soon. Not sure how I'm going to run them. On the one hand, Redcliffe is a smaller city so it would make a nice contrast if it had a vastly larger Freehold. On the other hand, it'd probably be more in keeping thematically with the Chronicle if they were even smaller than the Brisbane group.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'm pretty comfortable with Freeholds sitting in the 20-40 Changeling range, because it makes it possible for me to have done a proper character write-up for every Changeling character around, plus some notable humans. I'm definitely with Firefly Night on that respect. One of my players is about to start running a game with a Freehold of 400 or so Changelings, so I guess I'll get to see how going in the opposite direction works out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-29T22:14:03-08:00","id":61318,"title":"","content":"I'd likewise be more likely to trend toward smaller freeholds than the one I mentioned above; a certain degree of flexibility in available extras was being aimed for (the PCs were, by popular vote, established characters with Wyrd 4 and a few years under their belts and I was still getting the hang of the whole political-organization thing) and the location was basically slated for a borderline-apocalyptic Fae incursion as part and parcel of half the local Monarchs being high-Wyrd members of the Lost Pantheon (with another two in hermitage in various out-of-the-way places and a fifth having turned into the aforementioned fledgeling Fae).<br /> <br /> That the freehold's members covered a fairly wide area was something of a mitigating factor, as was the fact that about a fifth of the Summer and Winter Courts were exiled to the city limits and the Courts' local traditions were heavily focused toward particular aspects of their respective bailiwicks, but I haven't got much of an excuse for the surfeit of Entitlements beyond the place's occupying a similar niche to New Lyonesse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T04:36:07-08:00","id":61462,"title":"","content":"I understand the ST desire for less NPCs of power, especially if the ST feels the need to write up and track details on each character. I just feel like a small (grade school class) size group of Changelings doesn't offer much room for internal politics and social intrigue. Especially if the NPCs are as set in their ways as PCs tend to be. I mean with thirty-is NPCs a PC group of 4 could pretty much set up whatever ruling faction they decide to support. I get that the small population can be as clique-ish as any grade school class, but is that really interesting enough to think of as politics or intrigue?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T07:14:44-08:00","id":61520,"title":"","content":"Not a ST, so I have not tested the question you are asking. But my guess would be you'd want to explore two things to get your answer.<br /> <br /> <b><i>First, how much ST work do you want to do?</i></b> How long has the Gentry been getting away with the Durance and Escape = the Lost cycle? If you are willing to have Lost who are over a hundred years old, that\u2019s a different population answer than if between recapture or death by old age the Lost population is lowering as often as someone escapes the Durance.<br /> <br /> It is tempting (to me) to say that additions are balanced by deaths and therefore the Lost Courts are very interested in latest escapees. They could pretend to be indifferent, but would track and question them in either case.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Second, what is the relationship of the Gentry \u2018problem\u2019 to the Real World? </i></b>Another way to say this is, since the Lost population is directly a result of Gentry manipulation, and since the ST decides how often the characters 'escape' the Durance.... you have some logic path to deciding how much base population of escapees you are dealing with.<br /> <br /> My thought on this is that the Gentry appear to be tremendously powerful. You might compare this power to wealth in the Real World. If The Wealthy lose a favorite servant, They are annoyed but They replace them. If They lose Their favorite painting/sculpture, They start to push buttons and pay people to find Their Treasure. Perhaps for this reason, I see various Kith being more likely sought after than others---when they escape (Fairest or Elementals might have much harder time escaping Durance). Setting all that in context, if you have 4 to 6 escapees every 7 years or so, it is a tiny trickle, something like the POW escapes of WWII (where I should note, almost all escapees were recaptured, rarely did they truly escape as we see in movies.)<br /> <br /> But we want to play a game with more than a trickle of drama.... so perhaps we can look at it this way: extra-ordinary folks attract notice of the Gentry, so escapes are more common than you might expect. The Gentry are also easily distracted by Other Drama. They don't have Their fingers much in the Real World. Result: taking mortals to the Gentry lands is statistically common (replacements are needed and the Gentry have a powerful need for new amusements), but escapes are rare.<br /> <br /> If the Gentry were attracted to mortals and acting on a 1 in 10,000 model. They\u2019d be kidnapping 3000 people from the USA a year. The statistics for USA quote over 800k missing persons a year. No wonder the Gentry trafficking problem isn\u2019t being noticed!<br /> <br /> If you then figure, just for someplace to start, that 1 in a 100 folks escape, you have 30 returns (new Lost arriving back in the world) a year in the USA.<br /> <br /> Now that does not seem like enough people to tell stories about, but wait, let\u2019s add some more layers.<br /> <br /> What if the USA has ten Lost regional courts? So each might see 3 new Lost a year.<br /> What if some Courts actually spend time making escapes easier, doing works in the Hedge, or providing infrastructure for a \u2018Lost Railroad\u2019 of freedom? Double those specific numbers could be a bonus in certain Courts. Or you could work with Nodes and Paths that have been hidden and protected that make some Railroads work better.<br /> <br /> Big Cities might be disadvantaged with more Gentry attention, more kidnappings, few if any escapees crossing the Hedge; small cities and towns might actually be the place the Lost escapees more often cross the Hedge. A Big City might also be a place where Gentry are more likely to reacquire escaped Lost.<br /> <br /> And what if rural locations then are Freeholds and smaller Courts that are important to the Big City Courts especially because they are more likely to get \u2018new blood\u2019?<br /> <br /> Regardless of whether you scale this up or down, the Lost community should be fairly stable, and narratives can expect very few Lost every few years to arrive. Maybe as a policy, not much fuss is made over new PCs, or maybe it just looks that way to the confused and traumatized new PCs.<br /> <br /> Small cities have small Courts, and more likely have active \u2018escapee politics\u2019.<br /> <br /> Small towns have Freeholds, and more likely have to be \u2018politic\u2019 about keeping new Lost that show up there. They record the latest Lost for the small city Courts. Who in turn advise the Big City Courts. At some point, the Courts make offers to Lost, like grants or scholarships from Universities competing for talent.<br /> <br /> So a small town of 30,000 may have a freehold of 30 Lost? But lots of visitors from Small or Big Courts who keep an eye on this \u2018Hedge Railroad\u2019 location?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Arref</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>......making magic since 1154 B.C.</i></span></span>","edited":{"userid":2062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","user":"Arref","date":". 10-09-2014, 12:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","name":"Arref","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T09:08:43-08:00","id":61580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GitariArt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=61462#post61462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand the ST desire for less NPCs of power, especially if the ST feels the need to write up and track details on each character. I just feel like a small (grade school class) size group of Changelings doesn't offer much room for internal politics and social intrigue. Especially if the NPCs are as set in their ways as PCs tend to be. I mean with thirty-is NPCs a PC group of 4 could pretty much set up whatever ruling faction they decide to support. I get that the small population can be as clique-ish as any grade school class, but is that really interesting enough to think of as politics or intrigue?</div> </div> </div> </div>30 to 40 individuals in a social group is plenty dynamic when it comes to matters of intrigue. Especially when you factor in the broader fairy community consisting of Hedge dwellers, goblin markets, fetches, ghosts and dream-critters. Also remember that changelings don't live on an island, there is an entire mortal world that they must maintain contacts and alliances with ... which most likely includes family members, witches, paranormal investigators and the enchanted. With Hedge travel and modern transportation being what it is, changelings also aren't isolated from other freeholds and Lost that scheme there. You can still have vast conspiracies that shake your game to its core with a relatively small changeling support cast. <br /> <br /> However, just as a matter of personal taste, I do not like constant, antagonistic intra-freehold intrigues as an accepted norm in my Changeling games. A freehold's purpose is to provide a safe haven to heal for the psychologically and spiritually tormented survivors of fairy malice. There has to be an expected level of trust, safety and structured support present. If the freehold's courtiers and motleys are all bickering and backstabbing each other ... what the hell is the point of joining a court and freehold at all? Might as well be Courtless and live off the grid. Freeholds filled with infighting are freeholds that fall apart. Banishment from a freehold is one of the worst punishments for one of the Lost in my games; Not only have you been thrown out of a support structure that can provide you with protection from Them, but now a bad reputation follows you around. What freehold wants to bring a quarrelsome and duplicitous courtier into its midst? They have enough problems to worry about (Loyalists, privateers, dream poison, hunters etc etc etc) without taking on a potential traitorous courtier who has problems working with others.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T11:21:46-08:00","id":61633,"title":"","content":"I started out at eighty-something, and has been reducing the numbers with every game I run. My last planned chronicle, which never came to fruition, had a freehold of 11 (PCs excluded).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/97-the-mg","name":"The MG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar97_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2460"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T12:50:02-08:00","id":61681,"title":"","content":"If freeholds/courts are sall in number and only realy used for a safe place to gather and get support, Shat are Motleys for?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T12:56:21-08:00","id":61685,"title":"","content":"I guess a more modern way to look at it is by using more contemporary terminology.<br /> <br /> A Court is your &quot;subculture&quot; (Hipster, Rocker, Hip Hop head etc etc), a freehold is your &quot;scene&quot; (a collection of local peeps that are all acquainted with each other at various levels and attend the same shows and hang out at the same spots) and a motley is your &quot;clique&quot; or your &quot;crew&quot; (your good friends that you hang out with all the time, have inside jokes with etc etc)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T16:56:30-08:00","id":61833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GitariArt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=61462#post61462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand the ST desire for less NPCs of power, especially if the ST feels the need to write up and track details on each character. I just feel like a small (grade school class) size group of Changelings doesn't offer much room for internal politics and social intrigue. Especially if the NPCs are as set in their ways as PCs tend to be. I mean with thirty-is NPCs a PC group of 4 could pretty much set up whatever ruling faction they decide to support. I get that the small population can be as clique-ish as any grade school class, but is that really interesting enough to think of as politics or intrigue?</div> </div> </div> </div>Everyone wants something, and sometimes they need help to get those things. Changelings in a small Freehold, at least in my case, rarely go after political power for political power's sake. Rather, they seek to cultivate friends and acquaintances that will be useful for them in their personal endeavours. The Queen of Autumn has a collection of Tokens, and is always looking to add to it. One of the Summer Court has the skill set to acquire those items for her. However, a bad break-up with a different Summer Court member has left the Queen not feeling too great towards the Summer Court, and the feelings are largely reciprocated (at least among that courtier's circle of friends, which the first Summer Courtier may or may not belong to). So the Queen will look for some way to exert some influence over the Summer Courtier, ideally through a member of her own Court who knows that Courtier (perhaps due to being in a Motley with him).<br /> <br /> But really, I feel the politics won't be that different. People still lie and plot and try to twist others into doing what they want. The King of Summer is just as likely to seek the ruination of someone who insulted him in front of a gathering whether he's in a large Freehold or a small one. Vices like Pride, Greed, and Lust all crop up within small groups, and can have all the more devastating effects due to the size of the group and the personal nature of the betrayals. Personally, I find the raw number of participants way less important when it comes to dynamic and conflicting political ambitions than strong personalities and characters.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GitariArt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=61462#post61462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If freeholds/courts are sall in number and only realy used for a safe place to gather and get support, Shat are Motleys for?</div> </div> </div> </div>Firefly Night's example works pretty well. There's also a memory I have of a month long school camp I once went on a long time ago. We were one house of one year of the school, so there were maybe 40 of us. We had to do a lot of teamwork exercises and the like, go on long hikes, build communal living areas, and so on, even though we certainly didn't all get along. That's your Freehold. It can accomplish feats that no one on their own can do, even with &quot;only&quot; a single class's worth of Changelings.<br /> <br /> The motley, on the other hand, would be your personal group of friends. They might be the people you work best with, the people you get along with, or just a collection of the people left over once everyone else is done sorting out their own friendship groups. Whatever. Point is, you stick together. Even within a small Freehold, different people are going to want different things. The motley give you allies you can fall back on who will (hopefully) have similar objectives to your own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T18:36:33-08:00","id":61904,"title":"","content":"I get that if there are only a few dozen changelings in a city of (presumably) at least tens or hundreds of thousands of other people, then the changelings quickly adopt an us verses them mentality and band together--especially of there are Vamps, Magi, mortals, etc. out to cause problems for the Changelings in addition to Gentry, Fetch, and Puppets. As described above, however, freeholds still don't seem all that different from Motleys. I don't always get along with or agree with everything my tight knit group of friend does, any more than we do with the larger community we all belong to. So, it sounds to me like I am being told that &quot;the 35 person freeholdis different form the 4 person motley because they are not the same&quot;. Mostly I assume the real answer is the two are separate as an excuse for PCs to be able to use up two Pledge slots and gain the double boons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-30T19:11:19-08:00","id":61926,"title":"","content":"PCs can fill out Pledge slots pretty quickly if they want, and it fills out fastest if you try to grab pledges with individuals rather than groups like a Motley. So not sure why that would be necessary.<br /> <br /> Your motley are your friends. You don't necessarily agree with them all the time, but they're your allies. They're the ones you can rely on to back you up. Your Freehold should do the same when faced with an external threat (the Fae generally, my games don't include any non-Changeling supernaturals), but not every issue is that big. If you have a problem with someone in the Freehold who is not in your Motley, then you should be able to rely on your Motley to back you up even if you can't rely on the entire Freehold. Just to take an easy example.<br /> <br /> I'm pretty baffled by the comment &quot;the 35 person freehold is different form the 4 person motley because they are not the same&quot;. A 35 person Freehold is different from a 4 person Motley for a fairly obvious reason, I would think. There are 35 people in the Freehold, and 4 in the Motley. That means that the Motley is a small group, and the Freehold is a bigger group. That's literally all the two terms mean in terms of Changeling anyway. If your Freehold is small enough and perfect enough that every single member is a close friend, then maybe there is no difference between the terms.<br /> <br /> The easiest way I can think of to describe that is to use your own example of close-knit friends within a community. You don't always get along with your friends, no. You don't always get along with your community either. But you see a reason to draw a distinction between the group you call your close-knit friends and the community at large. The same distinction can probably be drawn to say why a Freehold is different from a Motley.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T05:58:08-08:00","id":62110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Firefly Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=61685#post61685\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Court is your &quot;subculture&quot; (Hipster, Rocker, Hip Hop head etc etc), a freehold is your &quot;scene&quot; (a collection of local peeps that are all acquainted with each other at various levels and attend the same shows and hang out at the same spots) and a motley is your &quot;clique&quot; or your &quot;crew&quot; (your good friends that you hang out with all the time, have inside jokes with etc etc)</div> </div> </div> </div>I like that, but let's massage the terms some more.....<br /> <br /> <b>Court</b> = large pledges/oaths, tied to ancient mantles/magic, supported by a large group of Changelings that may have many small conflicts, but have promised to put the Court above them, extremely hard to revoke or break pledge without Dire Consequence<br /> <br /> <b>Freehold</b> = modest pledges, tied to a place, invented by strangers but fitting your goals for a time<br /> <br /> <b>Motley</b> = small pledges personal to the crew/clique","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Arref</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>......making magic since 1154 B.C.</i></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","name":"Arref","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-31T10:57:52-08:00","id":62315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arref</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=62110#post62110\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like that, but let's massage the terms some more.....<br /> <br /> <b>Court</b> = large pledges/oaths, tied to ancient mantles/magic, supported by a large group of Changelings that may have many small conflicts, but have promised to put the Court above them, extremely hard to revoke or break pledge without Dire Consequence<br /> <br /> <b>Freehold</b> = modest pledges, tied to a place, invented by strangers but fitting your goals for a time<br /> <br /> <b>Motley</b> = small pledges personal to the crew/clique</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Thanks for bringing up the Oath aspect of the changeling psyche in relation to changeling social structure, because I feel it is an important facet of this conversation.<br /> <br /> Changelings, for the most part, are all pretty much borderline and crazy. They struggle with their perceptions and emotions, and due to the victimization that they have experienced, have learned that trusting others can lead to a horrible end. Opening themselves to any sort of outside influence, especially when it concerns other fairy people, runs a serious risk when it comes to their well-being and sanity. Luckily, they have the ability to organize themselves through the use of arcane promises that provide tangible benefits and punishments concerning the sanctity of the pledge. The fact that they have a limited number of pledges they can swear to keeps their circle of trust relatively small. After all, the likelihood of dealing with an oathbreaker rises the more people are in on a promise. For most changelings, I'd imagine that divulging that level of trust (even if enforced by magic) to a group large enough that a significant portion of it is effectively anonymous would be nightmarish. Nothing frightens a changeling more than a fairy face that they do not recognize. That stranger could be a Loyalist, a privateer, or just an insane changeling who will bite your face off and leave your body to be eaten by goblin dogs off in the Hedge.<br /> <br /> I imagine that trust increases with Clarity and with Wyrd (after all, you can swear more oaths), which is why more established changelings join extended social circles like Entitlements, which have a much broader and expansive membership than a standard freehold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-01T19:37:39-08:00","id":63244,"title":"","content":"Huh, I am genuinely surprised. I really was thinking of courts on a very different scale. When thinking about Changelings and what kinds of stories to tell, I thought about groups that suffer from t PTSD and alienation: people in the military and victims of human trafficking, as well as oppressed, outside groups like transgender people. Also, I was considering some particularities of Changelings:<br /> 1) since they harvest emotions and don't cause damage (or much damage) to the human population, they are not limited to being an apex predator like a vampire<br /> 2) there are weird (wyrd) things happening with Time and the Hedge-- sometimes your minute in Fae lands is 60 years in the mortal realm, sometimes your 120 years in Faery is an hour in the mortal realm. This would create some strange things in terms of Changelings returning: why wouldn't people from different times be showing up? I know the book has a sentence in it that claims no one before the 20th cen. will come out of the Hedge, but I won't let that ruin my fun. In short, there is a random influx and outflow of persons from the Hedge and there has been for a very long time. Since my game is set in the U.S., a percentage of my Changelings are native American and some are from a precolonial time period.<br /> 3) Fetches:What percentage of the population are fetches? It could be very, very high, or very, very low, who is to know? If you insist that Fetches are infertile, then in the U.S., that's about 6 million people. But that can't tell the whole story-- maybe that young mother was snatched right after her baby was born, so she's not infertile, she just only planned on having one child. When people become Lost, it's often an invisible crime. If a lot fewer Changelings make it out than are taken, then there are many fetches living their lives perhaps never aware of their fetch status.<br /> <br /> So all these ideas were milling around and I decided that for my game, Changelings in the United States were between one half of one percent and 1% of the total U.S. population: so no less than about 1,570,000 So a small group, but not tiny. So in my central setting of Athens, Ohio, there are maybe 100 to 200 Lost. The local court, Hawk Woods Court of the Midwest Territories (in the old growth forest in Ohio) has about a thousand subjects, although not all of them congregate at the salamander earthwork barrow mound-- there are two Duchies (Salt and Copper Duchies) that serve the Midwest Territories. I've got about 20 Changeling Territories mapped on North America.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-01T21:10:12-08:00","id":63277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quynce</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=63244#post63244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This would create some strange things in terms of Changelings returning: why wouldn't people from different times be showing up? I know the book has a sentence in it that claims no one before the 20th cen. will come out of the Hedge, but I won't let that ruin my fun. In short, there is a random influx and outflow of persons from the Hedge and there has been for a very long time. Since my game is set in the U.S., a percentage of my Changelings are native American and some are from a precolonial time period.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the notion that time is more Wyrd than many suspect and stranger than the other WOD predators may be able to perceive.<br /> <br /> There is always something timeless about the Fae... and making it a hinge point of your campaign is a nice feature. Certainly it is not an area much explored in the rule set.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Arref</b><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\"><span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>......making magic since 1154 B.C.</i></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2062-arref","name":"Arref","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-01T21:48:25-08:00","id":63288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quynce</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=63244#post63244\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2) there are weird (wyrd) things happening with Time and the Hedge-- sometimes your minute in Fae lands is 60 years in the mortal realm, sometimes your 120 years in Faery is an hour in the mortal realm. This would create some strange things in terms of Changelings returning: why wouldn't people from different times be showing up? I know the book has a sentence in it that claims no one before the 20th cen. will come out of the Hedge, but I won't let that ruin my fun.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u2026Anachronisms greater than a century tend not to show up because the connections those people could have followed have either died off or changed enough to be unrecognizable. You get back to Earth from Arcadia (without Fae assistance) by way of your memories of Earth leading you to that place mostly as you remember it; your children growing up is one thing, but your great-great-grandchildren are a different matter, and even without having been kidnapped by fairies I can point to the local main-street intersection as a thing I wouldn't recognize just a decade ago. It may have worked differently in pre-industrial times where the familiar features of one's day-to-day life didn't change overmuch, but the last century alone has been a storm of activity to such a degree that it's a surprise if you find anyone whose kidnapping predates the advent of powered flight.<br /> <br /> This means that, for the most part, if you find a freshly-escaped changeling who can't name a president after Taft, either they're a loyalist or they're suspiciously lucky. Plot complications abound.<br /> <br /> (Though now I'm imagining some family in the deep wilderness who routinely find themselves missing and then no-longer-missing their seventh sons.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-03T09:12:49-08:00","id":64050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Huh, I am genuinely surprised. I really was thinking of courts on a very different scale. When thinking about Changelings and what kinds of stories to tell, I thought about groups that suffer from t PTSD and alienation: people in the military and victims of human trafficking, as well as oppressed, outside groups like transgender people. </div> </div> </div>It's totes a YMMV sorta deal. However, there are certain implications that need to be taken into account if you decide to have a large population of magical people in any given city.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1) since they harvest emotions and don't cause damage (or much damage) to the human population, they are not limited to being an apex predator like a vampire </div> </div> </div>As is the main conceit in the hidden world subgenre of fantasy, the World of Darkness setting has a certain expectation of supernatural secrecy as its modus operandi. Humanity is kept &quot;in the dark&quot; as it were when it concerns the existence of the fairy world, and since there is no equivalent of the Mists in this edition of changeling, it is assumed that this ignorance is either due to a deliberate, multi-tiered conspiracy on the part of the Courts (which would require a vast level of influence and resources) or that there aren't enough changelings to cause more than a blip on the &quot;Weird Shit is Going Down&quot; radar.<br /> <br /> I'd personally go with the latter choice because, to quote Benjamin Franklin, &quot;Three men can keep a secret if two are dead.&quot;<br /> <br /> Even with the Mask, each changeling leaves a pretty big magical footprint on their landscape. A population of over two million magical creatures (fetches included), possessed of some pretty blatant magical powers, wouldn't go unnoticed for long, as not all of them will have access to Vainglory 5 or be ruthless/canny enough to silence every mortal who witnesses fairy magic. I'd imagine in such a setting, the existence of fairies and their antics would be an accepted (and perhaps unproven) part of metaphysical belief, instead of being considered make-believe.<br /> <br /> A simple, mechanical, fix would be to give every changeling the Hidden Life merit found in Rites of Spring. This would explain how changelings manage to have such an entrenched infrastructure and remain hidden from the perceptions of mortals. Honestly, I was a bit sad to see that there was no &quot;Mists&quot; equivalent in this rendition of Changeling, as I always found it to be a poignant and bittersweet mechanic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","user":"Firefly Night","date":". 02-03-2014, 12:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-03T17:38:38-08:00","id":64453,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Firefly Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=64050#post64050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Even with the Mask, each changeling leaves a pretty big magical footprint on their landscape. A population of over two million magical creatures (fetches included), possessed of some pretty blatant magical powers, wouldn't go unnoticed for long, as not all of them will have access to Vainglory 5 or be ruthless/canny enough to silence every mortal who witnesses fairy magic. I'd imagine in such a setting, the existence of fairies and their antics would be an accepted (and perhaps unproven) part of metaphysical belief, instead of being considered make-believe.</div> </div> </div> </div>I may be wrong about this, it's been a while since I read the relevant passage, but don't mortals tend to rationalise their exposure to the Fae away pretty quickly? I was pretty sure the reason why using Contracts in front of mortals is a Clarity hit is because the mortals rarely accept that it is actually magic, causing the Changeling to wonder if they are really the Fae creatures they think they are, or just insane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-03T20:37:09-08:00","id":64546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=64453#post64453\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I may be wrong about this, it's been a while since I read the relevant passage, but don't mortals tend to rationalise their exposure to the Fae away pretty quickly? I was pretty sure the reason why using Contracts in front of mortals is a Clarity hit is because the mortals rarely accept that it is actually magic, causing the Changeling to wonder if they are really the Fae creatures they think they are, or just insane.</div> </div> </div> </div>There isn't any magical impetus behind this rationalization. It is just natural that people would explain something that makes absolutely no sense whatsoever using rationalization instead of just jumping into &quot;OMG FAIRIES! GRAB THE SALT!&quot;<br /> <br /> However, if such fairy nonsense was more commonplace, people may just have to change their woldview.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T05:10:20-08:00","id":64753,"title":"","content":"Between most Fetch not really being aware of their magic nature and The Mask, I don't see how normals can easily pick up on fae. Changeling magic is rarely flashy and attention grabby like a fire ball and it requires no somatic or verbal components, so its even hard for a group of changelings to know who set a contract off. A mortal cop cuffs a Bright One and turns away, the Bright One casts the 2sd clause of Separation and gets away, all the cops knows is the perp got away, his most reasonable answer is the perp was an escape artist with a bit of wire. It's only one example, but I feel that te formula fits across the vast majority of contract use.<br /> Then there is that preservation thing. If chngelings are twitchy about everyone else oitentially finding them out, then they would at most times act as cautiously with their magic as possible.<br /> But mistakes do happen, hence myths, fairy tales, urban ledgends, alien abduction stories, and many fringe mystic and religious beliefs. All derived from normals having seen magic they had to try and justify in some manner. And even if normals did not naturally misinterpret and/or explain away the supernatural, then wouldn't one of the reasons to have a court be for the court to take steps to both mislead exposed normals and offer a sort of witness relocation to changelings that got caught, so to speak.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I also post a long form story, based on a Changeling: The Lost campaign at: <a href=\"https://www.fanfiction.net/s/10018173/1/Great-Fight-A-Twilight-Tommy-Tale\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.fanfiction.net/s/1001817...ght-Tommy-Tale</a><br /> I hope you will enjoy it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2020-gitariart","name":"GitariArt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T09:27:32-08:00","id":64915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GitariArt</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/55108-courting-new-ideas?p=64753#post64753\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Between most Fetch not really being aware of their magic nature and The Mask, I don't see how normals can easily pick up on fae. Changeling magic is rarely flashy and attention grabby like a fire ball and it requires no somatic or verbal components, so its even hard for a group of changelings to know who set a contract off. A mortal cop cuffs a Bright One and turns away, the Bright One casts the 2sd clause of Separation and gets away, all the cops knows is the perp got away, his most reasonable answer is the perp was an escape artist with a bit of wire. It's only one example, but I feel that te formula fits across the vast majority of contract use.<br /> Then there is that preservation thing. If chngelings are twitchy about everyone else oitentially finding them out, then they would at most times act as cautiously with their magic as possible.<br /> But mistakes do happen, hence myths, fairy tales, urban ledgends, alien abduction stories, and many fringe mystic and religious beliefs. All derived from normals having seen magic they had to try and justify in some manner. And even if normals did not naturally misinterpret and/or explain away the supernatural, then wouldn't one of the reasons to have a court be for the court to take steps to both mislead exposed normals and offer a sort of witness relocation to changelings that got caught, so to speak.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you have over 2 million individuals possessed of varying levels of sanity with the ability to perform obvious magical feats and no mystical amnesia to cover their tracks, &quot;isolated incidents&quot; will become much more common. Hence my statement of the need for a vast conspiracy to cover up all the fairy hijinks that will inevitably go down if the population of active changelings is that high. As for changeling magic being &quot;rarely flashy,&quot; I will have to disagree with you. Contracts of Elements, Fang and Talon, all the Eternal Season contracts, Mirror, Hours, Animation, Artifice and Stone (just to name the ones off the top of my head) are all incredibly flashy and attention grabby. People figure things out pretty fast, and speaking as a mortal human, I am confident of the fact that if there was REAL magic going down at such a scale we would figure it out pretty fast. It wouldn't be long before YouTube was flooded with iphone videos of dudes wreathed in flame, pulling out lamp-posts and running up the sides of walls.<br /> <br /> At the scale of millions of changelings ... their influence would be bigger than &quot;myths and fairy tales.&quot; At only thousands of changelings, coming in under the radar is a bit more feasible.<br /> <br /> However, don't let it be said that this is all BADWRONGFUN ... cuz I am sure it is all fun. I just think at the levels we are talking about, there would have to be some sort of cover up in place intentionally keeping humanity in the dark.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T17:25:46-08:00","id":65252,"title":"","content":"My campaign is set in a city of about 260,000 and I\u2019ve established the local freehold to have approximately 86 members; though I\u2019ve only detailed around a third that number. And yes, I do have it established that the courts amicably hand off power each solstice and equinox (though spring is now half way through and the Summer Court was almost completely annihilated in a recent botched attack, so there are some concerns as to whether or not they\u2019ll be able to take power come June 21st).<br /> <br /> The books pretty clearly state that there is no higher authority above the level of the freehold. I always assumed that the similarities in the court structure from one freehold to the next were the result of traditions passed down (and spread) from the courts\u2019 four founders. It\u2019s been some time since I gave Lords of Summer a thorough reading, but I don\u2019t recall anything contradicting the variance indicated in the core Changeling book. I recall it stating several times that the information it contained was just representative of a TYPICAL freehold.<br /> <br /> Speaking of Lords of Summer, it has information on several titles often held in certain courts and the useful services that they provide to their court in particular and to the freehold in general. However, it doesn\u2019t have as much to say about what the actual monarchs do; though in my experience, any time you have a group of more than a dozen people for ANY reason, it\u2019s a good idea to have someone in charge or there\u2019s gonna to be bedlam.<br /> <br /> So that\u2019s my perspective on some of the questions that have been raised in this thread, now I have of my own. Can someone direct me to where it discusses courts being ruled by pairs of people? I can\u2019t recall reading this anywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1912-vorzakk","name":"Vorzakk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1912_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"26"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-04T18:02:46-08:00","id":65278,"title":"","content":"In terms of higher authority, I can think of a few references. One of the Morpheans in Dancers in the Dusk can grant a lot of power to someone, but it won't last. One example given is that it could give a Changeling the power to unite Freeholds across Europe (or something) into a vast Empire, only for it collapse as if it had never been not long afterwards. Given the occasionally feudal trappings of a Freehold, I can see some potential for fun stories in a Changeling Emperor trying to bind together a lot of Freeholds (Fae-like activity though, so it will all likely go horribly wrong).<br /> <br /> In Equinox Road there's also the Entitlement called... something about the Gilded Torc. Whatever it was, it was only eligible for Court Leaders, and those that joined found that the Entitlement rules as a democratic council that make decisions for all of the Freeholds of their members (or something along those lines).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-05T10:39:39-08:00","id":65806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So that\u2019s my perspective on some of the questions that have been raised in this thread, now I have of my own. Can someone direct me to where it discusses courts being ruled by pairs of people? I can\u2019t recall reading this anywhere </div> </div> </div>.<br /> <br /> This was in reference to a &quot;home brew&quot; style of Court dynamics, not anything that was detailed in any of the books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-06T16:31:03-08:00","id":66832,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So that\u2019s my perspective on some of the questions that have been raised in this thread, now I have of my own. Can someone direct me to where it discusses courts being ruled by pairs of people? I can\u2019t recall reading this anywhere </div> </div> </div>When writing up my first court, I used Fear Maker's Promise, the module as an inspiration. They describe a King and Queen for each Season, as well as at least one other mover and shaker for each. As I mentioned earlier in this forum, I liked the aesthetics of pairs sharing power. Thematically, I find the game full of dualities and doubles: Mask and Mien, Changeling and Fetch, Before and After Durance. Sometimes I think of it like a presidential election-- the candidate gains power and support by having an running mate.<br /> <br /> P.S. I'm only using the core book and the Winter, Autumn, and Spring supplements, so I am admittedly working with less &quot;cannon&quot; than others are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-06T16:48:05-08:00","id":66843,"title":"","content":"Freehold structure is a pretty loose and variable thing, and the idea of ruling pairs is actually pretty great. I'm pretty sure it's just as canonical, if a good bit less common, as single rulers.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-07T05:01:48-08:00","id":67119,"title":"","content":"It <i>is</i> a cool idea and I didn't mean to imply that I thought it was 'wrong'. I actually thought it sounded very approropate to the setting, which was why I sincerely thought I'd missed something somewhere. : )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1912,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1912-vorzakk","user":"Vorzakk","date":". 02-07-2014, 08:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1912-vorzakk","name":"Vorzakk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1912_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"26"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}